  "sections": [
    {
      "key": "quickFacts",
      "source": "glaser",
      "sourceUrl": "https://www.aquariumglaser.de/en/25-perchlike-fishes/steatocranus-gibbiceps-2/",
      "html": "<p>S. gibbiceps grows to about 10 cm in length. It looks very similar to S. casuarius, but in S. casuarius the scale center is dark, while in S. gibbiceps it is light. S. gibbiceps is also more slender. The frontal hump is only moderately pronounced, but males develop a very elongated dorsal fin.</p>"
    },
    {
      "key": "waterConditions",
      "source": "glaser",
      "sourceUrl": "https://www.aquariumglaser.de/en/25-perchlike-fishes/steatocranus-gibbiceps-2/",
      "html": "<p>Although they inhabit very oxygen-rich water in nature, adaptation to aquarium life is not a problem for them. The water in the Congo is soft, but not overly acidic, so water composition requirements are also easily met.</p>"
    },
    {
      "key": "diet",
      "source": "glaser",
      "sourceUrl": "https://www.aquariumglaser.de/en/25-perchlike-fishes/steatocranus-gibbiceps-2/",
      "html": "<p>Any common fish food is eaten, but it must not be too fatty (no Tubifex and the like), because this leads to intestinal diseases, which can be fatal.</p>"
    },
    {
      "key": "behaviour",
      "source": "glaser",
      "sourceUrl": "https://www.aquariumglaser.de/en/25-perchlike-fishes/steatocranus-gibbiceps-2/",
      "html": "<p>Humphead cichlids should be kept at least in pairs, in sufficiently large aquariums also in groups, where there is always something going on. They are relatively peaceful, although the immediate area of the breeding burrow is fiercely defended and individual space is also otherwise demanded.</p>"
    },
    {
      "key": "reproduction",
      "source": "glaser",
      "sourceUrl": "https://www.aquariumglaser.de/en/25-perchlike-fishes/steatocranus-gibbiceps-2/",
      "html": "<p>Steatocranus are cavity breeders with parent families.</p>"
    },
    {
      "key": "notes",
      "source": "glaser",
      "sourceUrl": "https://www.aquariumglaser.de/en/25-perchlike-fishes/steatocranus-gibbiceps-2/",
      "html": "<p>Plants are not damaged, but occasionally they are dug up or bitten away when the animals get into breeding mood.</p>"
    }
  ],
